<h2>The two-motion architecture</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>First motion.</strong> A joint petition is filed in the family court within whose jurisdiction the marriage was solemnised, the parties last resided together, or the respondent (or, for a wife petitioner, she herself) resides. Both spouses appear, their statements are recorded, and the court satisfies itself that consent is free and the statutory separation of one year is made out.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The interregnum.</strong> Section 13B(2) fixes a window: the second motion may be moved not earlier than six months after the first and not later than eighteen months. The six-month floor is the &ldquo;cooling-off&rdquo; period — waivable post-Amardeep Singh where the court sees nothing left to cool.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Second motion.</strong> Both parties appear again and confirm that consent subsists. The court makes such inquiry as it thinks fit — typically verifying the settlement&rsquo;s performance — and passes the decree of divorce.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Amardeep waiver in practice</h2>
<p>Delhi family courts apply the Amardeep Singh factors as a checklist. A waiver application should demonstrate, with documents:</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Typical proof</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Separation beyond the statutory period plus six months</td><td>Separate rental agreements, utility records, pleadings in earlier cases</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Failed attempts at reconciliation</td><td>Mediation reports from court-annexed centres, counselling records</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Settled ancillary issues</td><td>The settlement deed covering alimony, custody, stridhan and pending litigation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Prolonged wait would only extend agony</td><td>Age of the parties, remarriage plans, duration of litigation</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Waiver remains discretionary. Where a settlement is partly performed, courts often decline waiver and instead hold the parties to the timetable, using the second motion as the compliance checkpoint.</p>

<h2>Where mutual-consent matters go wrong</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Consent withdrawn mid-stream.</strong> The remedy is not to force the decree — it cannot be forced — but to have drafted the settlement so that payments and withdrawals of cases are staged against each motion, leaving neither side exposed.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Vague settlement clauses.</strong> &ldquo;All disputes stand settled&rdquo; invites fresh disputes. Name every case by number, every payment by date and mode, every article of stridhan by list.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Jurisdiction and appearance slips.</strong> Both parties must appear at both motions (video-conferencing is permissible with leave; powers of attorney are accepted only narrowly). An NRI party should plan appearances or seek VC directions in advance.</div>
</div>

<h2>Timelines a client can expect in Delhi</h2>
<p>With a complete settlement and a successful waiver, a mutual-consent divorce can conclude in a few weeks from first motion. Without waiver, the realistic span is seven to nine months from filing to decree, assuming both parties remain cooperative. Compared against a contested petition — which commonly runs for years through trial and appeal — Section 13B remains the most economical route out of a dead marriage, provided the settlement is built to survive second thoughts.</p>
<div class="note">This walkthrough describes the general procedure for information. It is not legal advice; jurisdiction, settlement structure and waiver prospects vary with the facts of each matter.</div>
